title skeleton -- the classic Golay thinning template pack, a mask reduced to its medial skeleton, GREEN
prompt autonomous loop -- baton prin recur: pick up the running thread, take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, prove and send it
think Grounded as Kyri in Radiant Style (KYRI.md, RADIANT_STYLE.md), then read REMEMBER, the itineraries by reference, REDS, and the top rows of session-logs/README to pick up the thread.
think Standfast first: crux/REDS.md read -- every living red closed, released, or resting as custody-gated errata (#75 waits on a deep-debride custody gate, never agent-closable). The line is clear for new constructive work.
think Running thread: Season G open-media. The Microkernel double-seat leads by Lindy yet its only ungated moves are research behind a web-fetch/license gate (surfaced, never crossed), so the loop grows Season G. The thin.rye log named the crux-first next sibling by name: the SKELETON template pack -- the classic 8-rotation thinning pair the general thin driver was BUILT to run. That is the crux: the driver reduces a shape as far as ONE pair carries it; a real skeleton wants the SET.
think Read thin.rye and hitmiss.rye for the exact API -- structure.Offset{dx,dy}, StructuringElement{offsets}, thin.thin_once(alloc, src, fg, bg), hitmiss.hitmiss_on_threshold as the one source of truth for the binary meaning. Designed image/skeleton.rye to OWN only the template geometry (two Golay base pairs -- an edge template A and a corner template B -- each in four rotations via rotate90, interleaved) and the multi-pair sweep loop; thin.thin_once owns the set-difference step, hitmiss the match, structure the gather, qoi the buffers. No new pixel math -- a sweep is eight thin_once calls routed through the pack.
think Chose the cruxes for the checkable room (council rota this lap: TWO_ROOMS, item 18 -- state only what the witness binds; do not overclaim exact geometry). The DEFINING crux is anti-erosion: a one-pixel-wide plus must SURVIVE skeletonize untouched where a plain erosion would delete it. Hand-verified before coding -- traced all eight templates against the plus center (fg_b fits in all four rotations but bg_b never fits, a straight neighbor always ON where the ground template wants OFF) and against every arm tip (only one on-neighbor, every fg needs >= 2), so no template matches; the plus is a fixed point in zero sweeps.
think For the block reduction I asserted STRUCTURAL properties the math guarantees -- strictly fewer pixels, non-empty (the anti-erosion property, a block never erodes away), a subset, a fixed point re-skeletonizing cannot move, sweeps > 0, sweeps <= initial foreground count -- rather than exact skeleton pixels, which would be a fragile false-RED risk. Two Rooms discipline: bind what is provable, name geometry I do not verify as unclaimed.
obs GREEN on the FIRST full metal run, no reds -- the hand-verification held exactly: plus survives untouched (0 sweeps), 5x5 block thins to a strictly smaller non-empty subset fixed point, convergence bound respected, QOI round-trip byte-for-byte, empty source refused by name, determinism in sweep count and pixels. tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0, plain 7-bit ASCII, living_docs_lint GREEN, living_card_ascii_witness GREEN.
obs Termination is a bound not a hope: a sweep of anti-extensive steps only removes pixels, so each productive sweep removes >= 1 foreground pixel; ceiling = initial foreground count + 1, the loop asserting it is never crossed -- the same discipline thin.rye holds one level up.
obs REMEMBER repointed single-stranded -- the live-edge bullet and the Git-nib block now name skeleton as the edge, thin folded into the prior-nib trail (no cairn -- a one-line repoint, no verbose stack shed). image/README.md seats the Skeleton (Golay pack) bullet after the Thin (to a skeleton) entry.
